{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261118T212306Z-ab9db0b7", "ts": "2026-11-18T21:26:12Z", "actor": {"worktree": "arrt", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "chore/agentic-patterns", "build": "3682719168c6"}, "body": {"base_commit": "feec165e99987f38f09e284a3b57b70890f22736", "base_tree": "ecd554f859898d7e94529f388833b9b0dbc3f54e", "head_tree": "5c67be590589c9c5c61e7e28cf24a6db96afeae2", "head_commit": "be64e099d06f44e713482a074270531c54044e04", "dispatch_commit": "be64e099d06f44e713482a074270531c54044e04", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["CLAUDE.md", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py", "docs/testing.md"], "files_changed": [".claude/rules/learnings/core.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/accessibility-spec.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-agentic-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/changes/agentic-patterns.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "CLAUDE.md", "arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py", "docs/testing.md"], "files_oracle": [".claude/rules/learnings/core.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/accessibility-spec.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-agentic-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/changes/agentic-patterns.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "holding()'s release() keeps the race #432's fix removed from stop(): two tests release straight after a click and then assert len(held) == 1", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to `holding` and its callers. Why it broke: the fix added the wait to stop() only, and this commit changed release() from calling stop() to clearing `holding_now` directly, so the wait never reaches release(). The mechanism stop()'s new comment names is 'a click returns once it is dispatched, before its fetch reaches the route handler'. It applies the same way to `test_a_second_press_while_the_read_is_out_opens_one_dialog` and `test_choose_its_player_and_edit_pressed_together_open_one_dialog`. Each calls release() right after its clicks, with no stop() before it. If the Edit read's route event has not been pumped by then, release() turns holding off, the read passes through unheld, and `assert len(held) == 1` fails on 0. That is #432's flake, now in two other tests. The window is narrower, because the second click's round trips usually pump the first read's route event. This is inferred from the code and was not measured. The builder's own re-break should settle it: run these two tests with every fetch delayed by 300 ms, the condition the plan and change record used to show #432 red. The fix is one owner: release() waits through the same bounded loop before it clears the flag. Move the wait into a helper that both stop() and release() call, or have release() call stop() again now that stop() waits. Then every caller of holding gets the wait, not only the two tests that call stop(). Learnings rule cited: 'When you fix a defect, make inputs the fix does not know fail by name, and grep the commit for the same shape'.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "holding()'s wait-until-held guard covers stop() only; release(), the exit two tests use alone, was rewritten to skip it", "recommendation": "Class, bounded to this helper: the race #432 named (a click returns before its fetch reaches the route handler, so clearing `holding_now` at once lets the request through unheld) belongs to every exit that clears `holding_now`, not to `stop()` alone. This diff fixed it in `stop()`, and changed `release()` from calling `stop()` to clearing `holding_now[0]` itself, so the wait does not happen there. `test_a_second_press_while_the_read_is_out_opens_one_dialog` and `test_choose_its_player_and_edit_pressed_together_open_one_dialog` call `release()` straight after their clicks and then assert `len(held) == 1`. Those two tests can still race, and the plan's 300 ms fetch-delay experiment would turn them red the same way it did #432. As a result the next flake in either test gets investigated from scratch, as #432 was. Fix: give the helper one point that clears `holding_now`, behind the wait. The simplest version has `release()` call `stop()` again, which costs nothing after an earlier `stop()` because `held` is already at least `after`. Then check the two release-only tests under the same 300 ms delay. This applies the core learning 'When you fix a defect, make inputs the fix does not know fail by name, and grep the commit for the same shape'.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "fid": "R-2"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 2, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "In the release-only tests, len(held) == 1 cannot see a duplicate read that arrives after release()", "recommendation": "This predates the diff. In the two tests that release straight after their clicks, a second /api/walls read routed after release() passes through without being appended, so `len(held) == 1` holds even if readingOnce's dedup broke. The `dialog.panel-dialog` count == 1 assertion is the behavioural guard that would still catch it, so no change is needed for correctness. If release() gains the wait from the finding above, consider also counting requests that pass through after release, so that the 'one read' half of these tests can fail.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: both governed_by dispositions are true of the diff", "recommendation": "No action. Norm 1 (a household reader at standing distance): the claimed exception, a dated stopgap expiring 2027-01-17 at the owner's ruling of 2026-11-18, is in the norm's own entry in accessibility-spec.md \u00a7 Direction, and owner-rulings.md records the ruling. Norm 2 (WCAG 2.1 AA on the curation browser): 'inapplicable because this plan changes no screen' holds, since nothing under arrt/src/arrt/http/static/ is in files_changed and the only browser change is a test helper. The two new Workflow norms (Delegation, Delegate verification) are born with a vetoable owner ruling recorded in owner-rulings.md and a norm-index row.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Goal 1 and Goal 2 coverage checks are clean", "recommendation": "No action. test-status reported current (tree-valid) for head tree 5c67be590589 after the in-flight run recorded, with no failures. verify-coverage was skipped because coverage_required is false. Every chunk deliverable of build-plan-agentic-patterns.md is present. Chunk 01 has the two Workflow rows plus one norm-index row, the Stopgap field on norm 1, and stop() waiting in holding(). Chunk 02 merges four core.md rules into one (one rewritten, three removed) and moves the CLAUDE.md paragraphs to docs/testing.md with one-line pointers. #441 and #442 are named in the change record. The moved CLAUDE.md prose is in docs/testing.md word for word, and the UX-walkthrough rule stays in CLAUDE.md with its pointer, so no rule was dropped. No dependencies were added.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "CLAUDE.md and docs/testing.md each say the other holds every rule's reasons, but the UX bullet's home is docs/ux-walkthrough.md", "recommendation": "CLAUDE.md's 'What each rule guards ... is `docs/testing.md`' and docs/testing.md's 'states the rules below in a line each' are both untrue of the 'Whether the UI is good to use' bullet. Nothing in docs/testing.md covers it, and the bullet already names docs/ux-walkthrough.md itself, so no reader goes to the wrong place. This is inert prose. No edit is wanted unless the lines are touched again for another reason, and then the pointer should say 'or the file the bullet names'.", "files": ["CLAUDE.md", "docs/testing.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The 'Testing rules that bite' bullets restate reasons that now live in docs/testing.md", "recommendation": "The pointer-plus-one-line form follows the owner's ruling (owner-rulings.md item 4) and the plan. Some bullets copy the reason as well as the rule (the Starlette lifespan, testament dropping -n), which is the shape the learning 'When a claim already lives in prose, verify it against the code and add a pointer, never a copy' warns can drift. I checked every bullet against docs/testing.md and none disagrees today. No edit is wanted. If a bullet is touched later, keep only the imperative and the pointer.", "files": ["CLAUDE.md"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "stop()'s `after` parameter has no caller that passes it", "recommendation": "All four callers use the default of 1. The parameter is cheap and its meaning is clear, so this is borderline premature generalization. Leave it, especially if release() ends up routed through stop() as the finding recommends.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "holding's release()-only callers keep the click-then-act race that stop() now waits out", "recommendation": "This is outside sustainability's goals; it is recorded here for the correctness reviewer to rate. The new comment in stop() says a click returns before its fetch reaches the route handler. The same window is open in the two tests that call release() straight after their clicks and never call stop(): test_a_second_press_while_the_read_is_out_opens_one_dialog and test_choose_its_player_and_edit_pressed_together_open_one_dialog. If the route event has not arrived by then, release() turns holding off with nothing held, the read passes through, and `assert len(held) == 1` fails. There is no regression, since the old release() was just as instant, but these are the same class of race #432 was. Scope: class. The fix is for release() to wait the way stop() does (e.g. release(after=1) sharing stop's wait loop), so every caller goes through one owner. Adding a stop() call to each test would not close the class.", "files": ["arrt/tests/browser/test_editing_a_wall.py"], "oid": "O-7"}], "duration_seconds": 600, "scope": "agentic-patterns", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02"], "base_reviewed": "feec165e99987f38f09e284a3b57b70890f22736", "record_lint": {"records": [".claude/rules/learnings/core.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/accessibility-spec.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-agentic-patterns.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/changes/agentic-patterns.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "CLAUDE.md", "docs/testing.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-agentic-patterns.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-18T21:23:10Z"}}

